\f0\fs24 \cf0 ==============================================================================\
CATALOG OF EGO NETWORK DATASETS FOR DIFFUSION MODELING & HOMOPHILY RESEARCH\
==============================================================================\
Research Objective: Replication of Smith, McPherson & Smith-Lovin (2014)\
Methodology: Ego-Alter Dyads, Name Generators, Homophily estimation\
Date Compiled: December 2025\
==============================================================================\
\
1. ESTUDIO LONGITUDINAL SOCIAL DE CHILE (ELSOC)\
------------------------------------------------------------------------------\
Region: Latin America (Chile)\
Website: https://dataverse.harvard.edu/dataset.xhtml?persistentId=doi:10.7910/DVN/LD4BPH\
Documentation: https://manual-elsoc.netlify.app/\
\
Characteristics:\
- Type: Longitudinal Panel (Annual).\
- Network Module Availability: Rotating modules.\
  * Wave 2 (2017)\
  * Wave 4 (2019) - Recommended for primary analysis\
  * Wave 6 (2022)\
- Name Generator: "Important Matters" (Exact replication of GSS/Smith et al.).\
- Network Size: Max 5 alters.\
- Key Alter Attributes: Sex, Age, Education, Relationship Type, Religion, \
  Political Ideology, Neighborhood residence.\
- Ego Attributes: Comprehensive SES, Health, Politics.\
\
Suitability: HIGH. \
This is the closest match to the US GSS methodology available in the Global South. \
Ideal for direct parameter comparison (Educational/Age/Sex homophily).\
\
==============================================================================\
\
2. SHARE (SURVEY OF HEALTH, AGEING AND RETIREMENT IN EUROPE)\
------------------------------------------------------------------------------\
Region: Europe (28 Countries) + Israel\
Website: https://www.uantwerpen.be/en/research-groups/csb/data/featured-datasets/share/\
\
Characteristics:\
- Population: Adults aged 50+ and their partners.\
- Network Module: "Social Network (SN)" module available in Waves 4, 6, and 8.\
- Name Generator: Multi-step saturation method ("Discuss important things" + \
  "Closest to you").\
- Network Size: Up to 7 alters (analysis usually capped at 6).\
- Key Alter Attributes: Role/Relationship, Gender, Residential distance, \
  Frequency of contact, Emotional closeness.\
- Note: Education of alters is sometimes proxy-inferred or less detailed than ELSOC.\
\
Suitability: HIGH (Specific Context).\
Excellent for modeling diffusion in aging populations. High-quality data \
validation, though the age restriction (50+) limits general population claims.\
\
==============================================================================\
\
3. GERMAN GENERAL SOCIAL SURVEY (ALLBUS)\
------------------------------------------------------------------------------\
Region: Europe (Germany)\
Website: https://search.gesis.org/research_data/ZA5282\
\
Characteristics:\
- Type: Repeated Cross-section (The German equivalent of the US GSS).\
- Network Module Frequency: Typically every 10 years (e.g., 2000, 2010, 2021).\
- Name Generator: "Important Matters".\
- Key Alter Attributes: Sex, Age, Education, Political preference.\
\
Suitability: HIGH.\
The most direct "sister survey" to the US GSS. Ideal for testing if US \
homophily parameters hold in a different Western cultural context.\
\
==============================================================================\
\
4. COMPARATIVE NATIONAL ELECTIONS PROJECT (CNEP)\
------------------------------------------------------------------------------\
Region: Global (30+ countries across 5 continents)\
Website: https://u.osu.edu/cnep/\
\
Characteristics:\
- Focus: Political attitudes and behavior.\
- Name Generator: "Political Discussion" (Who do you talk to about politics?).\
- Network Size: Max 5 alters.\
- Key Alter Attributes: Relationship type, Frequency of discussion, \
  Perceived political agreement. (Demographic attributes like Age/Edu vary \
  by country wave).\
\
Suitability: MEDIUM/HIGH (For Political Diffusion).\
While it focuses on political networks rather than generic "confidants," \
it is the largest harmonized dataset for studying information diffusion \
mechanisms globally.\
\
==============================================================================\
\
5. CHINESE GENERAL SOCIAL SURVEY (CGSS)\
------------------------------------------------------------------------------\
Region: Asia (China)\
Website: http://cgss.ruc.edu.cn/English/About_CGSS/Overview.htm\
\
Characteristics:\
- Type: Repeated Cross-section.\
- Name Generator: Often uses "Spring Festival Greeters" (Who did you exchange \
  greetings with at New Year?) as a culturally valid proxy for core ties.\
- Access: Restricted (often requires application via CNRDS).\
\
Suitability: MEDIUM.\
Valuable for non-Western context, but the name generator is methodologically \
distinct from the "Important Matters" generator, requiring careful adjustment \
of imputation models.\
\
==============================================================================\
\
6. CANADIAN GENERAL SOCIAL SURVEY (GSS - Canada)\
------------------------------------------------------------------------------\
Region: North America (Canada)\
Website: https://www23.statcan.gc.ca/imdb/p2SV.pl?Function=getSurvey&Id=30687\
\
Characteristics:\
- Cycles of Interest: Cycle 22 (2008), Cycle 27 (2013), Cycle 35 (2020).\
- Topic: "Social Networks" and "Social Identity".\
- Name Generator: Estimates of friend counts + "Who would you turn to" (Resource/Support).\
- Access: Public Use Microdata Files (PUMF) often suppress detailed network \
  structure for anonymity; full network data usually requires Research Data \
  Centre (RDC) access.\
\
Suitability: MEDIUM (Due to access).\
High-quality data, but difficult to access the raw ego-alter dyads required \
for Smith et al. replication without physical presence in a Canadian RDC.\
\
==============================================================================}